  <h2>Why the punch list is a billing document</h2>
  <p>
    A punch list looks like a to-do list, but for a subcontractor it is the last gate before the
    final pay application. Most subcontracts hold retainage — typically 5% or 10% of every progress
    payment — until the work is complete and accepted, and “accepted” in practice means the punch
    list is closed and verified. On a $486,200 contract with 10% retainage, that is $48,620 sitting
    behind a list of diffuser alignments and missing escutcheons. Treating the list with the same
    discipline as the <a href="/templates/aia-g702-g703/">pay application</a> is how you get that money
    in weeks instead of months.
  </p>
  <p>
    The template is built around that fact. Every item carries the fields a verifier needs to close
    it, the Excel version counts what is still open, and the sign-off block turns the finished list
    into a document you can attach to the certificate of substantial completion.
  </p>

  <h2>Field by field</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>Location / room</strong> — floor, room number and grid line if the item is above a ceiling.
      Location-first ordering matches how the walk physically moves and lets one crew close a whole corridor
      in a pass.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Item</strong> — what is wrong <em>and</em> what fixes it. “RG-14 rattles” sends someone
      to look; “Return grille RG-14 rattles at high speed; add gasket and re-secure” sends someone with
      the right parts.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Trade / responsible</strong> — the trade for a multi-trade list, the person for your own
      list. Sort on this column to hand out work.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Priority</strong> — High for safety, water or weather exposure or anything blocking another
      trade; Med for anything that must close before final; Low for cosmetic. Without a priority, crews
      close the easy items first and the roof flashing waits.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Status</strong> — Open, Ready for Review, Complete. The middle status exists so the person
      who fixed the item is not the person who closes it.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Noted / due / completed</strong> — three dates. Days open computes from them in Excel, and
      the due date turns red when it passes without the item complete.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Verified by</strong> — the GC superintendent, owner’s representative or architect who confirmed
      the fix. An item without a verifier is not complete, whatever the status column says.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2>A worked example</h2>
  <p>
    The completed example is a mechanical subcontractor’s list from the owner–architect punch walk
    on a medical office building on November 5, 2026, reported as of November 12 — twelve items
    across two floors and the roof. Five are complete and verified by the GC superintendent, three
    are fixed and waiting for review, and four are still open. The four High items are the ones you
    would expect: a missing fire-caulk seal at a waste penetration, a missing condensate trap on
    RTU-2, curb flashing at RTU-4 and a missing hanger on a duct run — the safety, water and code
    items, each with a shorter due date than the cosmetic ones. One item refers back to a change
    order: the AHU-3 condensate drain added under CO-003 needs a cleanout and a label, which is how
    a change order’s scope shows up on a punch list two months later.
  </p>
  <p>
    Look at the status counts in the header: 12 on list, 4 open, 3 ready for review, 5 complete.
    That line is the whole conversation with the GC — what is left, what is waiting on them, and
    what is done — and in the Excel version it recalculates every time a status changes.
  </p>

  <h2>Pre-punch, owner punch and closeout</h2>
  <p>
    The most effective use of this template is before the official walk. Walk your own scope with
    your foreman a week ahead, list everything you would flag if you were the architect, and close
    it. Items you find and fix yourself never appear on the GC’s list, never get a due date set by
    someone else, and never hold retainage. Then use a second list for the items the owner’s walk
    produces.
  </p>
  <p>
    When the last item is verified, the list feeds three closeout documents: the
    <a href="/templates/certificate-of-completion/">certificate of substantial completion</a>, which
    records the punch list count and the date it must be closed by; the final pay application, which
    bills the retainage; and the final lien waiver that goes with it. Track the walk date, the
    inspection and the day the last item closed in your
    <a href="/templates/daily-report/">daily report</a>, and put the open count on the agenda in
    your
    <a href="/templates/meeting-minutes/">meeting minutes</a> every week until it reads zero. Our guide
    to
    <a href="/blog/punch-list-management-for-subcontractors/"
      >managing punch lists as a subcontractor</a
    >
    covers the walk itself in more detail.
  </p>

  <h2>Common mistakes this list is built to prevent</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Items described as a location only — “Exam 210” — with nothing about what is wrong.</li>
    <li>No priority, so the cosmetic items close first and the water items wait.</li>
    <li>
      The fixer marking their own work complete, then the verifier reopening it — with no record of
      either.
    </li>
    <li>Accepting items outside your scope without a note; they become your retainage problem.</li>
    <li>Losing the photos — take one when the item is noted and one when it is fixed.</li>
    <li>
      Closing the list without signatures. A verified, signed list is what supports the retainage
      release; a spreadsheet that says “complete” is an opinion.
    </li>
  </ul>
